    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d342">

                <pb facs="0569" n="455" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg455"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15 Arms Truce/39: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-09-24T22:00:00+01:00">September 24, 1931—10
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 24—8:45 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>162. At meeting of the Third Committee this afternoon Madariaga declared that
                    while his Government was prepared to vote in favor of any proposal which the
                    Committee would accept unanimously he would suggest as a more farsighted and
                    elastic measure a proposal whereby the Assembly should recommend to all nations
                    participating in the Disarmament Conference a 10 per cent budgetary reduction on
                    armament expenditures covering the period of 1 year.</p>
                <pb facs="0570" n="456" xml:id="pg456"/>
                <p>In view of our previous attitude on budgetary limitation<anchor n="63"
                        xml:id="d342-f1"/> unless I receive instructions to the contrary I shall
                    maintain entire reserve if this proposal takes the form of a resolution.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d342-f1" n="63" xml:id="d342-f1-content">
                <p>For the attitude of the United States on budgetary limitation as expressed in the
                    Preparatory Commission for the Disarmament Conference, see, for example, <ref
                        target="frus1926v01#pg_94"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1926, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp. 94</ref>–<ref
                        target="frus1926v01#pg_95">95</ref> and <ref target="frus1926v01#pg_97"
                        >97</ref>–<ref target="frus1926v01#pg_98">98</ref>; and <ref
                        target="frus1927v01#pg_172"><hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, 1927, vol.
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp. 172</ref>–<ref
                        target="frus1927v01#pg_173">173</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
